+        // `new` call) rather than re-extracting args by hand -- it already
+        // knows how to bind the block's locals and run the constructor call.
+        //
+        // NOTE: this builds a SEPARATE instance of the superclass itself,
+        // not one bound as `fn.moduleClass`'s own `this` -- so a `self`-style
+        // alias the superclass captures (e.g. `scala.runtime
+        // .ClassValueCompat`'s own `self =>`) resolves to that throwaway
+        // instance, not this module, and virtual dispatch on it (e.g.
+        // `computeValue`) won't find this module's own override. A more
+        // correct fix was tried (running the superclass constructor against
+        // an `InterpretedInstance` bound as `fn.moduleClass` itself) but had
+        // to be reverted: it made EVERY module access whose class extends
+        // some superclass with real constructor args return a real
+        // `InterpretedInstance` instead of `ModuleValue` -- a far bigger
+        // blast radius than intended, which broke unrelated, previously-
+        // working macro derivations (observed: upickle's own `ReadWriter`
+        // derivation regressed to a `StackOverflowError`). Left as a known
+        // gap rather than re-attempted broadly EXCEPT for this one, curated,
+        // well-known JDK-compat shim class (not user code, so no unrelated
+        // module can be affected): a module extending `ClassValueCompat`
+        // directly (e.g. `scala.reflect.ClassTag`'s own private `cache`
+        // object) genuinely NEEDS its virtual `computeValue` override
+        // reachable -- there's no other correct behavior here at all, since
+        // `computeValue` is abstract on `ClassValueCompat` itself and only
+        // ever gets a real body from a module like this overriding it
+        // (reached through http4s's `uri"..."` literal macro's own
+        // `ClassTag`-caching call chain). Scoped by the super-called class
+        // itself (not by `fn.moduleClass`'s name/shape), so it can't
+        // silently start matching some unrelated module.
+        case Some(parentTree)
+            if (parentTree match { case Block(_, e) => e; case t => t }) match {
+              case Call(superFn, _) => superFn.symbol.isConstructor && superFn.symbol.owner == requiredClass("scala.runtime.ClassValueCompat")
+              case _ => false
+            } =>
+          val instance = InterpretedInstance(fn.moduleClass, env)
+          val withThis = bindThis(env, fn.moduleClass, instance)
+          val (leadingStats, callTree) = parentTree match
+            case Block(stats, expr) => (stats, expr)
+            case other => (Nil, other)
+          leadingStats.foreach(interpretTree(_)(using withThis))
